.TH PHASORSTD_INI 3 "August 2026" "Phasor 4.0.0" "Phasor Standard Library 4.0.0"
.SH NAME
phasorstd_ini \- Phasor INI file parsing and generation functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBinclude "std/ini.phs";\fR
.PP
.B ini_read(content)
.B ini_write(data)
.B ini_read_entry(content, section, key)
.B ini_write_entry(content, section, key, value)
.B ini_read_section(content, section)
.B ini_write_section(content, section, entries)
.B ini_has_section(content, section)
.B ini_has_entry(content, section, key)
.B ini_remove_section(content, section)
.B ini_remove_entry(content, section, key)
.B ini_empty(content)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This module is quite simple, it reads and writes INI configuration data. Every function in this module
works purely on strings: it takes INI data (text) as input and, for the write functions,
returns updated INI-formatted text as output. None of these functions touch the filesystem thus 
work fine while sandboxed; to work with an INI file on disk, read it with \fBfread()\fR (see \fBphasorstd_file\fR(3)),
pass the contents through this module, and write the result back with \fBfwrite()\fR.
.PP
The parser recognizes \fB[section]\fR headers, \fBkey=value\fR entries, and \fB;\fR or
\fB#\fR comments (a semicolon or hash only starts a comment at the beginning of a line or
after whitespace, not inside a value). Entries that appear before any \fB[section]\fR header
belong to an unnamed section with an empty-string name. Blank lines and surrounding
whitespace around keys and values are ignored.
.SH TYPES
.TP
.B IniFile / IniSection
A parsed INI document is represented as a struct (conventionally named \fBIniFile\fR) whose
field names are the section names (the unnamed section, if any, uses the key \fB""\fR) and
whose field values are themselves structs (conventionally named \fBIniSection\fR) mapping
each entry's key to its string value. There is no fixed declared \fBIniFile\fR/\fBIniSection\fR
type to include; these structs are built and consumed dynamically, with whatever section and
key names are present in the data.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BR ini_read (content)
Parse INI text into a struct.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
INI-formatted text
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
An \fBIniFile\fR struct: one field per section, each holding an \fBIniSection\fR struct of
that section's key/value pairs (see TYPES)
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_write (data)
Serialize a struct back to INI text.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBdata\fR 12
An \fBIniFile\fR-shaped struct, typically from \fBini_read()\fR
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
INI-formatted text. Non-string entry values are converted with the same rules as
\fBto_string()\fR (see \fBphasorstd_type\fR(3)).
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBdata\fR is not a struct
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_read_entry (content, section, key)
Read a single value directly out of INI text, without building a full struct.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
INI-formatted text
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name (use \fB""\fR for the unnamed section)
.IP \fBkey\fR 12
Entry key
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The entry's string value, or an empty string if the section or key does not exist (there is
no way to distinguish a missing key from a key whose value is genuinely empty; use
\fBini_has_entry()\fR first if that distinction matters)
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_write_entry (content, section, key, value)
Set a single value in INI text, creating the section and/or key if needed.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
Existing INI-formatted text (may be an empty string to start a new document)
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name to write into; created at the end of the document if it does not already exist
.IP \fBkey\fR 12
Entry key; created if it does not already exist, otherwise its value is overwritten
.IP \fBvalue\fR 12
New value. If not already a string, it is converted with the same rules as \fBto_string()\fR.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The updated INI-formatted text
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_read_section (content, section)
Read an entire section out of INI text.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
INI-formatted text
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
An \fBIniSection\fR struct of the section's key/value pairs, or null if the section does not
exist
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_write_section (content, section, entries)
Replace an entire section in INI text (or add it if it doesn't exist).
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
Existing INI-formatted text
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name to replace or add
.IP \fBentries\fR 12
A struct whose fields become the section's entries, replacing any entries the section
previously had. Non-string field values are converted with the same rules as
\fBto_string()\fR.
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The updated INI-formatted text
.PP
.B Throws:
Runtime error if \fBentries\fR is not a struct
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_has_section (content, section)
Check whether a section exists.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
INI-formatted text
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if the section exists, false otherwise
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_has_entry (content, section, key)
Check whether a key exists within a section.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
INI-formatted text
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name
.IP \fBkey\fR 12
Entry key
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if the section and key both exist, false otherwise
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_remove_section (content, section)
Remove an entire section from INI text.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
INI-formatted text
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name to remove
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The updated INI-formatted text with the section removed. If the section did not exist, the
text is returned unchanged (except for having been re-serialized, which may normalize
whitespace and comments away).
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_remove_entry (content, section, key)
Remove a single key from a section.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
INI-formatted text
.IP \fBsection\fR 12
Section name
.IP \fBkey\fR 12
Entry key to remove
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
The updated INI-formatted text with the entry removed. If the section or key did not exist,
the text is returned unchanged (aside from re-serialization).
.RE
.PP
.TP
.BR ini_empty (content)
Check whether INI text is empty or contains only whitespace.
.RS
.PP
.B Arguments:
.RS
.IP \fBcontent\fR 12
Text to check
.RE
.PP
.B Returns:
Boolean true if \fBcontent\fR is empty or contains only whitespace, false otherwise. This
does not parse the content as INI; it does not detect, for example, a document that contains
only comments.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Reading and Writing a Config File
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/ini.phs";
include "std/file.phs";

var content: string = fread("config.ini");
var config: any = ini_read(content);

puts(config.database.host);
puts(config.database.port);

// Round-trip: modify one entry and write the file back
var updated: string = ini_write_entry(content, "database", "port", "5433");
fwrite("config.ini", updated);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Direct Entry Access
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/ini.phs";

var doc: string = "[server]\enhost=localhost\enport=8080\en";

if (ini_has_entry(doc, "server", "port")) {
    puts(ini_read_entry(doc, "server", "port"));  // "8080"
}

doc = ini_write_entry(doc, "server", "timeout", "30");
doc = ini_remove_entry(doc, "server", "host");
puts(doc);
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B Building a Document from Scratch
.PP
.nf
.RS
include "std/ini.phs";

var doc: string = "";
doc = ini_write_section(doc, "app", { name: "MyApp", version: "1.0" });
doc = ini_write_section(doc, "app", { name: "MyApp", version: "1.0", debug: "true" });
puts(doc);
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
Every function in this module is a pure string transformation; none of them read or write
files directly \(em combine them with \fBfread()\fR/\fBfwrite()\fR (see
\fBphasorstd_file\fR(3)) to persist changes to disk
.IP \(bu 2
Because the write functions re-parse and re-serialize the entire document, round-tripping
through any \fBini_write_*()\fR function normalizes formatting (spacing around \fB=\fR,
blank lines between sections) and drops comments
.IP \(bu 2
Comment characters (\fB;\fR and \fB#\fR) only start a comment at the start of a line or after
whitespace; a semicolon or hash inside an unquoted value is treated as part of the value
.IP \(bu 2
.B ini_read_entry()
cannot distinguish "key not found" from "key found with an empty value"; use
\fBini_has_entry()\fR if that distinction matters
